@* Covers the attribute splatting of the element: everything written as a plain HTML attribute lands on the
   rendered tag, and the class and the style the component builds itself are merged with the ones splatted
   through a dictionary rather than replacing them. *@

<BitElement data-val-test="bit">I'm a element</BitElement>

<BitElement Class="from-parameter" Style="color: red;"
            @attributes="@(new Dictionary<string, object>
                           {
                               ["class"] = "from-attribute",
                               ["style"] = "font-weight: bold;",
                               ["dir"] = "rtl",
                               ["id"] = "from-attribute",
                               ["aria-label"] = "from attribute",
                               ["tabindex"] = "3",
                           })">
    I'm a merged element
</BitElement>

<BitElement class="lowercase-class" style="color: blue;" id="lowercase-id" tabindex="4">
    I'm a lowercase element
</BitElement>

@* An enabled element must keep a disabled attribute of its own: the component only writes that attribute
   while it is disabled itself, since writing it as false would remove the splatted one instead. *@
<BitElement Element="button" disabled="true">I'm a self-disabled element</BitElement>

@* The same holds of the aria attribute, and of the tab stop the component takes away from a disabled element:
   an element the component was not asked to disable is left with whatever the page wrote on it. *@
<BitElement aria-disabled="true">I'm a self-aria-disabled element</BitElement>

@* An href is only useful splatted, and it is the one splatted attribute a disabled element cannot keep: an anchor
   with an href is focusable of itself and follows that href on the enter key whatever the tab order says. *@
<BitElement Element="a" href="https://bitplatform.dev/">I'm a link</BitElement>

<BitElement Element="a" href="https://bitplatform.dev/" IsEnabled="false">I'm a disabled link</BitElement>

@* An area is the other tag that is a hyperlink of its own, and the one with no box of its own for the pointer
   events of the disabled class to turn off, so the href is all there is to take away from a disabled one. *@
<BitElement Element="area" href="https://bitplatform.dev/" alt="I'm an image map link" />

<BitElement Element="area" href="https://bitplatform.dev/" alt="I'm a disabled image map link" IsEnabled="false" />

@* A boolean is the one attribute value that is not written as its text on a plain element: true writes the
   attribute with no value at all and false writes none of it, which is how a boolean splatted onto one of the
   names the component resolves itself has to come out too. *@
<BitElement aria-disabled="@false" data-plain-attribute="@false">I'm an element with false attributes</BitElement>

<BitElement aria-disabled="@true" data-plain-attribute="@true">I'm an element with true attributes</BitElement>
